Subcutaneous hematoma from minor crush mechanism to dorsal foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Dorsal foot soft tissue mass, Low-energy crush mechanism, Intact distal perfusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-027
Reason for Visit: Expanding, painful mass on top of the foot following a crush injury, requiring urgent evaluation for vascular compromise and occult fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, low-energy crush injury to the foot causing swelling and tight, aching pain. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for underlying midfoot structural fractures and clear the patient of acute foot compartment syndrome before the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: A heavy cart rolled over my foot; it is throbbing deeply and swelling up fast on the top.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a low-energy compression event where a heavy wheeled cart rolled slowly over the foot 3 hours ago. This resulted in a localized collection of fluid forming a palpable soft tissue mass on the dorsum of the foot. The patient describes a dull aching pain and a distinct sensation of skin tightness stretched over the affected area. The swelling is poorly defined but distinct from a generalized ankle or midfoot joint effusion. There is a gradual appearance of visible ecchymosis spreading slightly dependent to the primary zone of injury. Pain is tolerable and allows for continued weight-bearing when barefoot.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but unable to tolerate enclosed footwear due to swelling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor workplace or environmental safety hazard requiring attention to prevent future incidents.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/78 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, resting the foot flat without distress but visibly swollen.
Physical Exam: Foot examination reveals a dorsal foot soft tissue mass and localized ecchymosis. Intact active movement is confirmed at the ankle and all metatarsophalangeal joints. Despite the localized swelling, there is no severe point tenderness directly over the metatarsal shafts or cuneiform bones. A strong dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable, indicating intact distal perfusion and no vascular compromise. Normal distal sensation and brisk capillary refill (<2 seconds) are noted in all toes. There is no pain out of proportion during passive stretch of the digits.
Environmental Observations: Injury occurred in a garage workspace; patient advised to wear sturdy footwear in this area.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subcutaneous hematoma to dorsal foot - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Metatarsal fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of severe bony point tenderness, lack of mechanical crepitus, and preserved weight-bearing ability.

Compartment syndrome of the foot - Ruled out due to: Soft plantar compartments, intact strong pedal pulses, normal digital sensation, and painless passive stretch of the toes.

Lisfranc injury - Ruled out due to: Intact plantar arch, negative plantar ecchymosis, and pain-free passive midfoot rotation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Crush injuries to the foot inherently demand a high index of suspicion for multiple metatarsal fractures and limb-threatening compartment syndrome. Bedside clinical evaluation definitively isolated the pathology to a superficial soft tissue hematoma, with fully intact neurovascular status and preserved structural stability allowing weight-bearing. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rule out surgical emergencies, establish immediate localized compressive therapy, and provide safe weight-bearing parameters overnight, which safely avoids an immediate, unnecessary presentation to an emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised standard RICE therapy. Prescribed short course oral NSAIDs to reduce acute localized inflammation. Plain radiographs of the foot are advised routinely tomorrow to definitively rule out subtle occult fractures of the underlying metatarsals or cuneiforms, despite the reassuring clinical exam.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear a supportive, stiff-soled shoe or post-op shoe when mobilizing. The rigid sole prevents the midfoot from bending during the walking cycle, significantly reducing mechanical stress on the bruised tissues and preventing sharp pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Post-operative rigid shoe, compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 days to review plain film radiographs and ensure swelling is receding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if the pain becomes sudden and excruciating, if the foot turns completely pale or cold, or if you develop numbness spreading into the toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Minor, non-displaced fractures can be masked by swelling and soft tissue pain; an x-ray is still required for definitive clearance.
Clinical Course: Swelling will peak at 48 hours. Bruising will spread to the toes and sole of the foot due to gravity. The lump will slowly dissipate over 2-4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you become entirely unable to put any weight on the foot, or if the skin feels dangerously tight and wooden.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A crush injury bursts small blood vessels under the skin. Ice and elevation prevent further bleeding into this tight space, reducing the pressure that causes the throbbing ache.
Actionable Steps: Get an x-ray of the foot tomorrow. Elevate the foot on a stool when sitting. Wear a stiff-soled shoe to prevent the foot from bending when you walk.

